1. The WeakAura Assignment Exporter

This is highly feasible and one of the most satisfying things you can build for a raid team.

    How it works: You will add an "Export" button to your React site that takes the raidAssignmentData JSON for a specific boss and converts it into a compressed string.

    The Challenge: WeakAuras are written in Lua. You will need to write a custom WeakAura that accepts a text input, decodes it, and translates it into a Lua table.

    The Path Forward: You can actually just export raw JSON from your website. Modern WeakAuras can use a library called json.decode (or similar implementations) to turn that string back into a readable table, which the WA then uses to show text on the screen based on the player's name.

2. The Discord Bot (Raid Events & /request)

This will be the biggest "quality of life" upgrade for your guild, period.

    How it works: You will build a separate Node.js script using the discord.js library. Because your web API is already built on Node and SQLite, the Discord bot can literally just talk to your existing localhost:3001 endpoints, or read the database directly.

    Your Instinct on Autocomplete: Spot on. Discord's API supports Autocomplete Interactions. As the user types /request flas..., the bot queries your items table (which has everything, not just what is currently in stock) and returns a clickable list.

    The Challenge: Discord's UI components (Embeds, Buttons, Select Menus) have strict formatting rules. Handling state in a chat app (like clicking "Sign Up" on an event) requires managing databases accurately to ensure no double-bookings.

3. WarcraftLogs (WCL) Sync & Graphs

This is visually impressive but will introduce you to a new way of fetching data.

    How it works: WCL uses GraphQL, which is different from the REST APIs (fetch('http...')) you've been using. You send a highly specific query asking for exactly what you want (e.g., "Give me the healing parses for player X on boss Y").

    The Path Forward: You will set up a cron job (a scheduled task) on your backend that pings WCL every night, parses the data, and saves it to your player_parses table.

    For the Graphs: You will drop a library like Chart.js or Recharts into your React app. You feed it your database arrays, and it instantly renders beautiful, toggleable line charts.

4. Battle.net Login & Character Claiming

I have great news for you: your instinct is wrong, this is actually much easier than you think!

    The Reality: Blizzard uses standard OAuth2 (the exact same protocol as "Sign in with Google" or "Sign in with Apple").

    How it works: You redirect the user to Blizzard's secure login page. If they log in successfully, Blizzard sends them back to your site with a secure token.

    The Magic: With that token, you can ping Blizzard's API and say, "Who does this token belong to?" Blizzard responds with their BattleTag and a list of all their WoW characters. You just match those characters to the names in your roster table. Libraries like passport-bnet handle 90% of the heavy lifting for you.

All four of these are incredible projects that will teach you widely used industry skills (Lua integration, Chatbot APIs, GraphQL, and OAuth2 Authentication).